<article class="grid max-w-5xl gap-6">
    {{ template "hero.gohtml" . }}

<h2>Trusted proxy mode</h2>
<p>Behind proxies, use <code>RealIPWithOptions</code> and list only your own proxies, as IP addresses or CIDR ranges. It only trusts forwarding headers when the remote address belongs to a configured proxy.</p>
<pre><code>r.UseAll(middleware.RealIPWithOptions(middleware.RealIPOptions{
    TrustedProxies: []string{"127.0.0.1/32", "::1/128"},
}))

ip := middleware.ClientIP(req)</code></pre>
<p><code>RealIPWithOptions</code> panics on an entry that is not an IP address or CIDR range; <code>TryRealIP</code> returns the error instead.</p>

<h2>No proxy</h2>
<p><code>RealIP</code> trusts no proxy: the client IP is the address of the direct peer, and forwarding headers are ignored, since any client can send them.</p>
<pre><code>r.UseAll(middleware.RealIP)</code></pre>

<h2>Headers</h2>
<p>The middleware understands <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>, <code>X-Real-IP</code>, and <code>Forwarded</code> when proxy headers are trusted. <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> is read from the right, skipping trusted proxies: the first address that is not a trusted proxy is the client, so a client cannot choose its address by sending a header of its own. <code>X-Real-IP</code>, and then <code>Forwarded</code>, are used when <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> is absent.</p>

<h2>Reading the client IP</h2>
<ul class="docs-list">
    <li><code>ClientIP(req)</code> returns the client IP, or the peer's IP when the middleware did not run.</li>
    <li><code>GetClientIP(ctx)</code> returns the stored client IP, or <code>""</code>.</li>
    <li><code>FromTrustedProxy(req)</code> reports whether the peer is a trusted proxy, so other forwarding headers can be believed.</li>
</ul>
<p>When the client differs from the peer, <code>RemoteAddr</code> becomes the client IP with port 0 (<code>203.0.113.10:0</code>) on the request the middleware passes on, so it still parses with <code>net.SplitHostPort</code>. The request itself is not changed: middleware outside it still sees the peer's address.</p>

<h2>Scheme, host and origin</h2>
<p><code>Scheme(req)</code> and <code>Host(req)</code> give the scheme and host a request arrived on, and <code>Origin(req)</code> both, such as <code>https://example.com</code>. They read <code>X-Forwarded-Proto</code> and <code>X-Forwarded-Host</code> (or <code>Forwarded</code>) only for requests from a trusted proxy. <code>SameOrigin(req, u)</code> reports whether a URL the client sent, such as <code>HX-Current-URL</code>, is an address on that host, and <code>SameOriginReferer(req)</code> returns the path and query of a <code>Referer</code> on it.</p>
<p><code>router.LocalURL</code> guards redirects to an address from a form or query against open redirects: it accepts paths on the site only, and returns the parsed URL.</p>
<pre><code>next := req.FormValue("next")
if _, ok := router.LocalURL(next); !ok {
    next = "/"
    if referer, ok := middleware.SameOriginReferer(req); ok {
        next = referer
    }
}
http.Redirect(w, req, next, http.StatusSeeOther)</code></pre>
</article>

